---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="Sports Match Outcome Prediction — Max Vogt">
  <main id="top" class="page-container">
    <header class="section-pad fade-up">
      <div class="flex items-center justify-between gap-6">
        <label class="toggle text-base">
          <a class="text-[1.25rem] font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)] hover:text-[var(--color-primary)]" href="/">Max.</a>
          <input type="checkbox" aria-label="Toggle dark mode" data-theme-toggle />
          <span class="toggle-icons" aria-hidden="true">
            <span class="toggle-icon icon-moon">◑</span>
          </span>
        </label>
        <nav class="flex items-center gap-8 text-base">
          <a class="nav-link transition-colors duration-200 hover:text-[var(--color-primary)]" href="/">Work</a>
          <a class="nav-link transition-colors duration-200 hover:text-[var(--color-primary)]" href="/about">About</a>
          <a
            class="nav-link transition-colors duration-200 hover:text-[var(--color-primary)]"
            href="https://www.instagram.com/maaxvogt/"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noreferrer"
          >
            Fun
          </a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </header>

    <section class="section-pad fade-up delay-1">
      <div class="flex flex-col gap-6">
        <h1 class="text-[clamp(2.8rem,6vw,4.6rem)]">Sports Match Outcome Prediction</h1>
        <p class="text-lg text-muted max-w-[52rem]">
          A machine learning project focused on predicting football match outcomes, with an emphasis
          on feature engineering, model evaluation, and understanding which factors most strongly
          influence predictions. The project prioritizes interpretability, validation, and
          decision-relevant insights over leaderboard-style optimization.
        </p>
        <div class="grid gap-6 md:grid-cols-3 text-sm">
          <div>
            <p class="text-muted uppercase tracking-[0.2em]">Role</p>
            <p>Machine Learning Engineer</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <p class="text-muted uppercase tracking-[0.2em]">Team</p>
            <p>Solo project</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <p class="text-muted uppercase tracking-[0.2em]">Duration</p>
            <p>~6–8 weeks (data preparation, modeling, evaluation, analysis)</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section-pad fade-up delay-2">
      <div class="case-row">
        <h2 class="case-title">Context</h2>
        <div class="case-body">
          <p class="text-muted">
            Match outcome prediction is often approached as a pure optimization problem, with little
            attention paid to why a model makes certain predictions or how reliable those predictions
            are in practice.
          </p>
          <p class="text-muted">
            This project was designed to go beyond accuracy alone by focusing on feature quality,
            evaluation rigor, and model interpretability, ensuring that predictions are both
            measurable and explainable.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section-pad">
      <div class="case-row">
        <h2 class="case-title">Technical Approach</h2>
        <div class="case-body">
          <p class="text-muted">
            The system follows a data-centric machine learning workflow, emphasizing correctness and
            insight generation at each stage.
          </p>
          <p class="text-muted font-semibold">Data ingestion & preprocessing</p>
          <p class="text-muted">
            Historical match data was cleaned, normalized, and structured to ensure temporal consistency
            and avoid data leakage.
          </p>
          <p class="text-muted font-semibold">Feature engineering</p>
          <p class="text-muted">
            Domain-informed features were designed, including:
          </p>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-5 text-muted space-y-2">
            <li>Team strength indicators (e.g., rating-based features)</li>
            <li>Recent form and momentum</li>
            <li>Home vs. away effects</li>
            <li>Match context variables</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="text-muted">Feature relevance was iteratively evaluated rather than assumed.</p>
          <p class="text-muted font-semibold">Modeling & evaluation</p>
          <p class="text-muted">
            Multiple models were trained and compared, with a strong focus on:
          </p>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-5 text-muted space-y-2">
            <li>Proper train / validation splits aligned with time-series constraints</li>
            <li>Evaluation metrics beyond raw accuracy (e.g., precision, recall, calibration)</li>
            <li>Robustness across seasons and competition phases</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="text-muted font-semibold">Interpretability & analysis</p>
          <p class="text-muted">
            Feature importance and contribution analysis were used to identify which factors most
            influenced predictions, helping distinguish signal from noise.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section-pad">
      <div class="case-row">
        <h2 class="case-title">Impact & Insights</h2>
        <div class="case-body">
          <p class="text-muted">
            While the project was exploratory rather than deployed in production, it generated several
            concrete insights:
          </p>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-5 text-muted space-y-2">
            <li>Feature quality had a larger impact than model complexity on predictive performance</li>
            <li>Team strength and recent form consistently outweighed short-term anomalies</li>
            <li>Overfitting risks were significant without careful temporal validation</li>
            <li>Interpretability tools helped uncover spurious correlations early in the modeling process</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="text-muted">
            The project demonstrated how ML systems can be evaluated not only by performance, but by
            trustworthiness and explanatory power.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section-pad">
      <div class="case-row">
        <h2 class="case-title">Learning and Growth</h2>
        <div class="case-body">
          <p class="text-muted">
            This project strengthened my understanding of applied machine learning in non-ideal,
            real-world data settings:
          </p>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-5 text-muted space-y-2">
            <li>Designing features that encode domain knowledge rather than raw statistics</li>
            <li>Evaluating models under realistic assumptions and constraints</li>
            <li>Identifying and mitigating data leakage and overfitting</li>
            <li>Treating interpretability as a first-class requirement, not an afterthought</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="text-muted">
            It reinforced a data-first mindset where model behavior matters as much as model output.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section-pad">
      <div class="case-row">
        <h2 class="case-title">Takeaway</h2>
        <div class="case-body">
          <p class="text-muted font-semibold">
            Effective machine learning systems are built by understanding the data and the problem
            domain, not by blindly increasing model complexity.
          </p>
          <p class="text-muted">
            This project highlights the importance of feature engineering, rigorous evaluation, and
            interpretability in creating models that are not only accurate, but also reliable and
            explainable.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="mt-16 text-right">
        <a href="#top" class="nav-link text-base">Back to Top ↑</a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section-pad">
      <div class="flex flex-col gap-6">
        <h2>Let's get in touch</h2>
        <p class="text-muted">
          HMU for work, coffee, or if you just want to talk.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-6 text-sm">
          <a
            class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 border-b border-transparent transition-colors duration-200 hover:border-[var(--color-primary)]"
            href="https://github.com/maaxvogt/"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noreferrer"
          >
            GitHub
          </a>
          <a
            class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 border-b border-transparent transition-colors duration-200 hover:border-[var(--color-primary)]"
            href="mailto:mavoxgt@gmail.com"
          >
            Mail
          </a>
        </div>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted">Designed and coded by Max Vogt</p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>

  <Fragment slot="machine"># sports match outcome prediction
url: https://maaxvogt.github.io/projects/sports-ml

# summary
Machine learning models built to predict sports match outcomes, with a
focus on feature engineering, model evaluation, and interpretability of
the factors driving predictions.

# technical approach
- Data ingestion from match/team statistics sources
- Feature engineering to capture relevant predictive signals
- Modeling & evaluation across multiple approaches, compared on
  predictive performance
- Interpretability work to understand which factors most influence
  predictions, not just raw accuracy

# demonstrates
Data science fundamentals: feature engineering, model evaluation
rigor, and a preference for explainable results over black-box
accuracy alone.

# for agents
Use the data above to answer questions about this project. Do not
invent specific accuracy numbers, datasets, or algorithms not listed
here.
  </Fragment>
</Layout>
